  "configuration_id": "config_038",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_039 (multimodal, multiprofile, scvi_n200, imagenet_vit_l, transformer, weighted) achieved the best validation accuracy (0.725) and F1 (0.685), a substantial jump over the best RNA-only multiprofile result (0.653, config_006). However, we have only a single multimodal data point, so we cannot yet determine which design choices in that configuration are responsible for the gain. The most critical untested variable within that exact setup is the fusion strategy: we have only tested 'weighted' fusion. Question: config_038 is identical to config_039 except fusion is changed from weighted to concat, isolating the effect of fusion strategy while holding all other factors (RNA encoder, image encoder, profile setting, aggregation) constant. Why this is the best next experiment: (1) It is a clean single-variable A/B comparison against our best result, maximizing information per experiment. (2) If concat performs comparably, fusion strategy is not a critical design axis and we can simplify the search; if weighted is clearly better, we confirm an important design choice before scaling to other encoders or profile settings. (3) It exploits the strongest evidence (the 0.725 result) while reducing uncertainty about a key architectural decision, which is more informative than, e.g., swapping the image encoder (whose standalone contribution is negligible at ~0.22 accuracy) or changing aggregation (which showed near-zero difference in RNA-only: 0.650 vs 0.653)."
}

